{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/825763-can-someone-explain-the-mage-time-of-judgement-novel","id":825763,"title":"Can someone explain the mage time of judgement novel?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-07T21:51:05-08:00","id":825763,"title":"Can someone explain the mage time of judgement novel?","content":"The novel version, featuring william albacastle, etc.<br /> <br /> I have no idea what this novel was talking about. While the vampire/werewolf novels had a coherent story, this didnt. Three characters are chosen for....something....we get cameos from characters like porthos who are supposed to be dead, except that they dont actually do anything other than impart cryptic warnings to the characters. The characters are killed, resurrected, and then killed again by some mysterious force without having achieved anything. The ending talks about some gibberish about &quot;ascending the spheres&quot; whatever that means and makes no mention of what happens to humanity or the universe after.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4880-question","name":"Question","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-08T01:45:15-08:00","id":825844,"title":"","content":"You cannot really explain Ascension, as once you're there human concerns are beyond you. As a whole the Ascension book and novel were pretty much a miss, as Mage neither had an impending doom clock, nor was Ascension something that needed explaining or a solution.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-09T10:36:14-08:00","id":826929,"title":"","content":"You reassure me that I chose wisely by not spending money on this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-10T14:20:43-08:00","id":827785,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/825763-can-someone-explain-the-mage-time-of-judgement-novel?p=826929#post826929\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You reassure me that I chose wisely by not spending money on this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good man, good man.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-16T19:26:51-08:00","id":831426,"title":"","content":"The basic gist of it is that the universe ran its course and came to its end, with the three characters serving a role in helping ease certain elements of the universe back into their correct place as part of the great cosmic oneness that they were mere reflections of and to then manifest as personifications of the primal Three letters that make up the fundamental elements of the universe before merging back into the Monad. There's actually quite a bit of mention of what happens to humanity and the universe that you've dismissed as gibberish - it's weird, esoteric, and frankly, a little clumsily handled, but it's there. Basically, it all returns to its original form. Mankind ceases to exist as we would recognize it, and so does the universe, but this is okay because mankind and the universe as we knew them were only pale reflections of the original macrocosm; even the cosmos itself was only one emanation - flawed and imperfect - of the macrocosm. The spheres, as raw emanations of divided cosmic force, reunite to return to nothingness and unity-in-nothingness, restoring the infinity of possibilities that the monad encapsulated and which was degraded by time and by the increasing sophistication of the emanations.<br /> <br /> Simple version? Universe merged back to its original state of unity; mankind ceased to exist as meaningfully distinct from anything else, and all is as it should be. The state of nothingness and raw pure potential is the best possible outcome for the universe under this model. It is the only Judgment day book to actually have a truly positive, not bittersweet or just outright bitter, ending. Like Mage at its best and its worst, the ending is all about potential, and that's kind of cool. Vampire was always a game about inevitability, horror, the decline of the world and of the self, with no real hope of escape, no real way out, other than a wing and a prayer. Werewolf was always a game about raging against a different inevitability, spitting in the eye of evil, and disembowelling it with your last breath to try and make a better world for your great-grandchildren. Mage was always, at its core, about possibility. The three ToJ novels reflect that, with Vampires all - more or less universally - dying a horrible death or entering torpor to die peacefully/perhaps one day wake, Werewolves literally all spending their last breath to try and disembowel the enemy that strangled them to death (and <i>possibly</i> succeeding, too, with that seed sprouting at the end), and Mage being nothing more and nothing less than the return of complete and unlimited potential to the cosmos, free of the constricting complex structures formed by the various emanations and cosmic paradigms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5307-loomer","name":"loomer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5307_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-25T05:14:59-08:00","id":836978,"title":"","content":"The Novel also literally calls out the Yozis, the most direct Exalted reference I have ever seen...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}